      "part": "worked_solution",
      "original_record": "history",
      "value": "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{3-y}+1\\,⟹\\,y=3-(x-1)^{2}\n$$\n\nThe function $y=0$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=2.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x).$ A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096470/assets/be799df2140d3a437bb311427dcae0e297cb6bd3844f88932157eb6ace6e7e59.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=0$ and leaves through the upper function $y=3-(x-1)^{2}.$ So, we have $0\\le y\\le 3-(x-1)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-(x-1)^{2}\\}.\n$$",
